Research digest for dmitrii
2026-11-03 to 2026-11-16 (inclusive, UTC)
Categories: cs.LG, cs.CL, stat.ML, cs.AI, cs.IR

15 recommended papers

1. DisMUSE: An Efficient Distributed Singular-Spectrum-Based Optimization Approach with Theoretical Convergence Guarantee
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10212v1

DisMUSE develops distributed Muon-style spectral optimization with Newton-Schulz and low-rank momentum communication, directly targeting convergence and communication-quality tradeoffs.

Why it matters to you: Strong match for spectral optimization, Newton-Schulz methods, low-rank communication, and training-efficiency tradeoffs.

2. Finite Spectral Depth Controls Gaussian Entrance Rank and Avoidance of the First Gradient-Flow Saddle
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05022v1

Analyzes how finite Newton-Schulz depth changes rank selection and enables matrix-factorization trajectories to bypass early saddles.

Why it matters to you: Directly relevant to Muon dynamics, rank selection, and spectral optimization theory.

3. ResQAdam: Residual-Preserving Adaptive Optimization for Memory-Efficient Quantization Aware Training
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08924v1

ResQAdam combines low-rank optimizer-state updates with residual sign information for memory-efficient quantization-aware LLM training.

Why it matters to you: Strong match for optimizer-state compression, memory-efficient training, throughput, and quality tradeoffs.

4. From What Is Used to What Is Missing: Rethinking Dynamic Rank in LoRA
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07349v1

GEAR uses tangent-normal geometry and spectral information to evolve LoRA rank according to task-relevant directions.

Why it matters to you: Strong match for dynamic rank allocation, LoRA geometry, and spectral adaptation.

5. Spectral and Subspace Dynamics of LoRA in Supervised Fine-Tuning and Reinforcement Learning
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08414v1

Studies singular-spectrum and subspace dynamics of LoRA in supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning, attributing differences partly to baseline subtraction.

Why it matters to you: Directly addresses how optimization regimes affect LoRA spectra and subspaces.

6. BiMuon: A Geometry-Aware Stochastic Bilevel Optimization Approach
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10188v2

BiMuon introduces geometry-aware stochastic bilevel optimization with Muon-style matrix updates and analyzes spectral imbalance.

Why it matters to you: Relevant to matrix optimization, Muon updates, curvature, and convergence theory.

7. Grokking Can Be Steered: Selecting Desirable Solutions By Choice Of Learning Rule
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.19477v1

Studies how learning rules, weight decay, and optimizer choices steer memorization-to-generalization behavior and effective circuit size.

Why it matters to you: Strong match for generalization, memorization, regularization, compressibility, and optimizer effects.

8. SaMA: Morpho Adaptation via Asymmetric Expansion of Kronecker Product
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04462v1

SaMA proposes a Kronecker-based PEFT parameterization whose effective rank scales more favorably with parameter budget and whose geometry is more optimizer-friendly.

Why it matters to you: Relevant to low-rank parameterizations, PEFT, rank scaling, and optimization geometry.

9. Efficient LLM Adaptation with Forward-Only Passes
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10610v1

Presents a forward-only method for selecting SVD components to reduce LLM weight rank with lower memory and greater speed than gradient-based search.

Why it matters to you: Directly relevant to singular-value-based compression, model adaptation, memory, and speed.

10. Direction or Magnitude? Learning Scalars over Task-Calibrated Directions
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17784v1

SCALAR performs PEFT by learning scalar coefficients over fixed task-calibrated directions, reducing trainable state and training memory.

Why it matters to you: Strong match for memory-efficient fine-tuning and quality-throughput tradeoffs.

11. The Behavioral Rate of Fine-Tuning: Compression as a Measure of Useful Information
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06598v1

Defines behavioral rate as the compressed adapter size needed to preserve fine-tuning behavior and studies how it depends on data, receiver model, and noise.

Why it matters to you: Especially relevant to low-rank compressibility, adapter information, singular directions, and corrupted training data.

12. Delayed Effects of Temporary Weight Decay in Muon Pretraining
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17876v1

Examines delayed effects of temporary weight decay in Muon-trained Transformers across several model scales.

Why it matters to you: Directly relevant to spectral/weight regularization, Muon optimization, and scale-dependent training behavior.

13. DictLLM: Post-training Compression of Large Language Model with Dictionary Kernels
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08082v1

DictLLM performs post-training LLM compression using shared SVD dictionaries, sparse coefficients, and factor-specific quantization.

Why it matters to you: Strong match for low-rank compressibility, singular spectra, quantization, and memory-quality tradeoffs.

14. Theoretical Analysis of Muon on Transformers for Next-Token Prediction
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04465v1

Provides a theoretical analysis of Muon for Transformer next-token prediction, including spectral margins, convergence, weight structure, and generalization.

Why it matters to you: Relevant to theoretical understanding of Muon, spectral structure, and LLM optimization.

15. LIRA: A More Interpretable Dynamic Loss-Informed Rank Allocation for Low-Rank Adaptation
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04239v1

LIRA allocates LoRA rank dynamically using interpretable second-order loss-trajectory signals under matched parameter budgets.

Why it matters to you: Directly relevant to optimization-aware rank allocation and interpretable LoRA behavior.

Limitations:
- Several abstracts are truncated, so some experimental details and limitations are unavailable.
- Repository availability and reproducibility were not checked.
- The selection is based on the supplied corpus and screening evidence, not a fresh web search.
- inspect:2611.10212: only question-relevant HTML passages were inspected, not the whole paper.

Fetched 4655 papers; screened 30.
